   132	## Tests
   133	
   134	- **Extractor**: the four tolerance fixtures (partial tail; malformed line skipped and counted; oversized line bounded; subagent turns dropped as deliberate classification), boundary-quote start, line-count mode, cross-directory ID search, empty/unparseable refusal.
   135	- **Supervisor**: semaphore init and consumed-marker (no refire after relaunch), `dont-restart` y/n paths, kill → extract → launch ordering (files-first verified), local retention old+new, task pre-seed as executable canaries (re-run per upgrade), ignition prompt contains path + elapsed-time line + task count.
   136	- **Trigger**: threshold crossing fires the skill exactly once.
   137	
   138	## Known holes
   139	
   140	- Pre-seed rides undocumented harness state; bounded by the ignition count-check and the per-upgrade canaries; the queues are the backstop.
   141	- Very-long-session material can predate the boundary window and every durable store; bounded by commit-as-you-go and the full-JSONL pointer.
   142	- ~~The 5-topics boundary is the retiring agent's judgment~~ — closed 2026-08-06: the boundary is mechanical (a 2500-word tail), so this hole no longer exists. What remains is the floor's size itself: a session whose live thread runs longer than 2500 words hands over a partial thread, bounded by the header's left-behind count and the transcript pointer.
   143	- A `dont-restart` handoff reaching a supervisor with no terminal cannot be answered. Closed 2026-08-06: the supervisor takes the non-relaunch branch rather than calling `input()`, which would raise EOFError before the consumed counter was recorded and leave the next supervisor re-firing on a stale handoff.
   144	- A supervisor restarting after a crash used to relaunch the session id in its state file. Closed 2026-08-06: a fresh start always mints a new id, since reusing one would launch against an existing transcript, and — if the supervisor died while its agent kept running — put two processes on one session id. Adoption is how a running session is picked back up.
   145	- The supervisor is a per-agent console process; if it dies, recycling stops until relaunched. Detectable since 2026-08-06 (user-asked): the supervisor stamps `last_poll_at` into its state file every ten seconds while watching, and `handoff-supervisor.py --check --agent <name>` reports liveness (exit 0 alive, 1 not). Both consumers use it — the skill checks before it stops working, so a dead supervisor yields a plain report to the user rather than a session hung forever waiting to be killed; the threshold hook stays silent when nothing is watching, so it cannot ask for a handoff nobody will act on.
     1	# git-gatekeeper build — slice plan
     2	
     3	Issue: [nedschorus#3](https://github.com/nedschorus/nedschorus/issues/3)
     4	
     5	Working material for the build of `scripts/git-gatekeeper.py`, the single
     6	program through which every change reaches `main` in nedschorus. This
     7	document decomposes that build into slices, fixes the boundary of the first
     8	one, and records the design points the specification deliberately leaves to
     9	the builder.
    10	
    11	**The specification is canonical, not this document.**
    12	[`docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md`](../cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md)
    13	(design-as-of 2026-07-24) states the contract; the build bindings in
    14	[`docs/issues/queue/3-gatekeeper-build-bindings.md`](queue/3-gatekeeper-build-bindings.md)
    15	(boss-walked 2026-07-30) supplement it with B1–B6. This plan only says what
    16	gets built in what order, and answers questions those two documents leave
    17	open. Where this plan and the specification appear to disagree, the
    18	specification wins and this plan is wrong.
    19	
    20	Author: choirmaster, session 1caf1c51 (first build task).
    21	
    22	## Why the order matters
    23	
    24	The gatekeeper is not an optimization; it is the project's only check-in
    25	lane. Until it exists, choirmaster is push-less by ruling
    26	([nedschorus#45](https://github.com/nedschorus/nedschorus/issues/45),
    27	2026-08-07): it commits to its own branch and a Mac-side agent merges after
    28	review. The interim was ruled explicitly as "until the git-gatekeeper
    29	provides the check-in lane." So the first slice is chosen for one property
    30	above all others: **it is the shortest path to a change actually reaching
    31	main through the program.** Everything that does not serve that goes later.
    32	
    33	## The five slices
    34	
    35	| Slice | Delivers | Spec tests | Retires |
    36	|---|---|---|---|
    37	| 1 **BUILT 2026-08-08** | Synchronous check-in end to end: screening → candidate → commit → push | T1, T2, T3, T9 | the manual merge lane, on the happy path |
    38	| 2 **BUILT 2026-08-08** | The entry checkpoint: `--import` and the `imports` query | T10, T11 | hand-recorded legacy imports |
    39	| 3 **BUILT 2026-08-09** | Concurrency: loser integrates over newer commits, real conflict refuses, retry cap | T4, T5, T6 | slice 1's `main-moved` refusal |
    40	| 4 | Worker lifecycle: `--no-wait`, detached worker, `status`, `cancel`, crash recovery | T7, T8 | slice 1's `unbuilt-option` refusal |
    41	| 5 | Enforcement surfaces: trailer-absence audit, branch-protection audit, repo git config, CLAUDE.md workflow lines | T12, B3c | the founding-window "boss watches every landing" guard |
    42	
    43	Not in any of the five, and deliberately so: the review-evidence check for
    44	the instruction-file class
    45	([nedschorus#31](https://github.com/nedschorus/nedschorus/issues/31), guard
    46	1). It needs an approval-evidence format that does not exist yet, and the
    47	in-session tamper guard
    48	([`.claude/hooks/instruction-file-guard.py`](../../.claude/hooks/instruction-file-guard.py))
    49	covers the same surface today. It becomes slice 6 when that format is
    50	designed.
    51	
    52	### Why the boundaries fall here
    53	
    54	- **Import is separable from check-in.** The import triple is three extra
    55	  fields, one extra copy step, and one extra trailer line — none of it on
    56	  the path a non-importing change takes. Splitting it keeps slice 1 from
    57	  carrying the legacy-repository fixture.
    58	- **Concurrency is the hardest logic in the design and is not needed to
    59	  land the first change.** Rebuilding a candidate over a moved main and
    60	  deciding clean-re-application versus real conflict is where the bugs
    61	  live. Slice 1 handles a moved main honestly — it refuses and teaches the
    62	  fix — and slice 3 upgrades that refusal into automatic integration. This
    63	  is the project's manual → script → automation ladder applied inside one
    64	  component.
    65	- **The worker lifecycle serves slow checks, and there are no slow checks
    66	  yet.** `--no-wait`, the detached worker, `status`, and `cancel` all exist
    67	  for a caller who does not want to block. In version 1 the pipeline is
    68	  screening plus construction plus a push; waiting is cheap.
    69	- **The audits are detection, not gating.** They catch the cooperative
    70	  residual (an agent pushing raw, an owner editing protection). Useful, but
    71	  they protect a lane that does not exist until slice 1 ships.
    72	
    73	## Slice 1 — synchronous check-in, end to end
    74	
    75	*BUILT 2026-08-08. `scripts/git-gatekeeper.py` with
    76	`scripts/git-gatekeeper-test.py`; every item below is in place. The
    77	gate is working but dormant: this box authenticates as `ubuntu-claude`
    78	and branch protection admits only `NedLern`, so nothing checks in to
    79	the real repository until open item 1 is settled.*
    80	
    81	**Built:** `scripts/git-gatekeeper.py`, command `check-in`, `--wait` only,
    82	`--import none` only.
    83	
    84	In scope:
    85	
    86	1. **Instant screening, complete.** Every form refusal in the
    87	   specification's catalog that a non-importing request can reach:
    88	   `malformed-field`, `missing-message`, `unknown-path`, `unchanged-path`,
    89	   `empty-change`, `unknown-base`, `base-not-on-main`, plus B2's
    90	   `unsafe-path`. Each refusal names the error, the specific facts, and the
    91	   exact next action (B5's verb-first phrasing), and touches nothing.
    92	2. **The digest** (specification field 8): SHA-256 over base id, sorted
    93	   path list, each path's new bytes, and the import triple. Screened
    94	   against history at submit, so an identical resubmit answers
    95	   `already-checked-in <commit>` having done no work.
    96	3. **The resolve-once request record** (B4c): every environment-derived
    97	   field — origin above all — is resolved during screening and written into
    98	   the workspace as the request record. Nothing downstream re-derives it.
    99	4. **Candidate construction** in
   100	   `$XDG_STATE_HOME/nedschorus-gatekeeper/<digest>/` (B4a): a clone at the
   101	   declared base with exactly the declared changes applied. Unchanged files
   102	   come from main, never from the caller's worktree.
   103	5. **The commit and the full trailer block**, including B6's
   104	   `Gatekeeper-agent` line.
   105	6. **The push**, happy path.
   106	7. **The advisory** (specification § Constructive guarantees): undeclared
   107	   modifications in the caller's worktree are noted in the reply and never
   108	   block.
   109	8. **B1's reply contract**: one JSON object on stdout; exit 0 success and
   110	   informational, 1 catalog refusal, 2 program defect.
   111	9. **Workspace sweep** on both endings.
   112	
   113	Out of scope, each with the slice that takes it: `--import` (2), `imports`
   114	(2), automatic integration over a moved main (3), `conflict` and
   115	`main-moving-too-fast` (3), `--no-wait` and the detached worker (4),
   116	`status` (4), `cancel` (4), the refusal record B4d (4), both audits (5),
   117	repo git config (5), CLAUDE.md workflow lines (5), review evidence (6).
   118	
   119	**Tests** — `scripts/git-gatekeeper-test.py`, matching the convention
   120	already in `scripts/`: standard library only, self-running, one PASS/FAIL
   121	line per case, non-zero exit on any failure. Cases: T1 every form refusal,
   122	each asserted to leave no side effect; T2 the happy path's four success
   123	guarantees with the trailer block asserted exactly; T3 the three digest
   124	properties (identical resubmit deduplicates, changed content digests fresh,
   125	metadata-only change does not); T9 the advisory; the `main-moved` refusal;
   126	and B3d's version-floor smoke assertion (Python ≥ 3.12, git ≥ the recorded
   127	floor — this box runs Python 3.14.4 and git 2.53.0).
   128	
   129	**The alternative boundary, considered and not chosen:** slice 1 could stop
   130	after screening and the digest — the whole pure core, no git writes, fully
   131	testable, roughly half the code. It was rejected because it delivers no
   132	check-in: the program would exist and the manual merge lane would still be
   133	the only way to main. The smaller slice is the fallback if slice 1 as
   134	scoped proves too large to land in one reviewable change.
   135	
   136	## Rewrite-policy classification for this slice
   137	
   138	Required by the rewrite policy (founding plan § Standing decisions): every
   139	piece of work that touches legacy material classifies the legacy features
   140	it touches. Two facts bound this slice's table.
   141	
   142	First, the specification already performed this classification at design
   143	time (§ Relationship to the legacy design); the table below restates those
   144	dispositions in the four-class vocabulary rather than re-deciding them.
   145	Second — verified 2026-08-07 — **the legacy checkout does not exist on this
   146	box.** `~/Projects/nedlern` is absent; only `~/Projects/nedschorus` is
   147	present. Slice 1 therefore reads no legacy material at all, and its
   148	classification is inherited, not fresh.
   149	
   150	| Legacy feature (`git-clean-slate-plan.md`, legacy `docs/working/proposed/`) | Class | Record |
   151	|---|---|---|
   152	| Workflow rules expressed as CLAUDE.md documentation | update-feature | Kept as documentation only, never enforcement — a Python program does not read CLAUDE.md, and different machines carry different copies. Lands in slice 5. |
   153	| Protection-as-lock | update-feature | Reduced from many-writer protection to one credential behind one program. Live since 2026-07-21. |
   154	| Three GitHub Apps | remove-feature | Multi-writer machinery; NC has one writer. |
   155	| Credential helper | remove-feature | Serves per-agent credentials, which NC does not issue. |
   156	| Per-agent branches | remove-feature | Ordinary changes use no branches. |
   157	| PR pipeline for ordinary work | remove-feature | Replaced by the single gate; PRs are not the ordinary path. |
   158	| Parking states | remove-feature | The four-state model has no parking; a stalled request is resubmitted, not parked. |
   159	| Minimal repo git config (`user.name`, `user.email`, `useConfigOnly`) | preserve-feature | Contract preserved, values re-derived and stated in the specification, not imported. Test-pinned in slice 5. |
   160	
   161	No consider-feature entries, so nothing goes to `legacy-feature-queue/`.
   162	
   163	## Design points this plan settles
   164	
   165	The specification leaves these to the builder. Each is a real fork, and
   166	each is recorded here as the answer the build will use.
   167	
   168	### D1 — Where the program reads main from
   169	
   170	The program clones `origin` into the per-digest workspace and builds the
   171	candidate there, at the declared base. It never uses the caller's worktree
   172	as the source of unchanged content, and it reads the caller's worktree
   173	exactly once, for the declared paths (specification field 1).
   174	
   175	Tests need this to point somewhere other than GitHub. Per B3a, pushing
   176	tests target a throwaway local bare repository, fresh per test. The program
   177	therefore takes the remote from an explicit argument that defaults to the
   178	invoking repository's `origin`, so a test can hand it a fixture path
   179	without any special test-only code path in the program.
   180	
   181	### D2 — Options not yet built
   182	
   183	Slice 1 does not implement `--no-wait`, `--import` with a value, or the
   184	`status`, `cancel`, and `imports` commands. Reaching one of them must not
   185	be an unnamed ending, and it must not exit 2 (B1 reserves exit 2 for
   186	program defects, so an argument-parser rejection would make a loop counter
   187	read a scoping decision as a gatekeeper bug).
   188	
   189	New catalog entry, `unbuilt-option`: a normal three-part refusal, exit 1,
   190	naming the option, the slice that builds it, and the available alternative
   191	("resubmit with `--wait`"). It is removed entry by entry as each slice
   192	lands, and is gone after slice 5.
   193	
   194	### D3 — The value of the `Gatekeeper-agent` trailer
   195	
   196	B6 requires `Gatekeeper-agent: <runtime/model>` on every commit, never
   197	omitted. The environment does not carry it: this box exposes
   198	`CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` and `AI_AGENT=claude-code_2-1-220_agent`, which
   199	name the runtime and its version but not the model, and the model is the
   200	half the fix ladder needs.
   201	
   202	The program therefore takes it as a required declared field, resolved once
   203	at screening like every other environment-derived value. The caller is the
   204	only party that knows which model it is. This is the cooperative class — a
   205	caller can declare wrongly — consistent with the design's honest-singleton
   206	stance: the gatekeeper records what it is told and never guesses.
   207	
   208	### D4 — A moved main, before slice 3
   209	
   210	Slice 1 pushes once. If the push is rejected because main moved since the
   211	declared base, the reply is a refusal named `main-moved`, carrying the
   212	files, the intervening commits, and the next action: update from main,
   213	rebase the work, resubmit. Nothing is left behind; the workspace is swept.
   214	
   215	`main-moved` retires in slice 3, where the same condition splits into
   216	automatic integration (clean re-application, the usual case) and `conflict`
   217	(the new main touched the same content).
   218	
   219	## Open — awaiting the user
   220	
   221	1. **The push credential.** Slice 1 ships fully tested against local bare
   222	   repositories, but it cannot check anything into
   223	   `nedschorus/nedschorus` from this box: the box is authenticated as
