     1	# Credential and hook bindings for pair #3 (git-gatekeeper) — ruled 2026-08-09
     2	
     3	Queued for the design rewalk. Destination:
     4	[nedschorus#3](https://github.com/nedschorus/nedschorus/issues/3), folded into
     5	the canonical spec `docs/cross-project/git-gatekeeper-design.md` at the walk.
     6	Source: the user's credential discussion with choirmaster, 2026-08-09 (session
     7	1caf1c51). These bindings amend the spec's enforcement periphery — the
     8	credential, the account layout, and the cooperative tier around the gate. The
     9	core contract (request/reply, digest, trailers, concurrency, states, error
    10	catalog) is untouched.
    11	
    12	Provenance note: items C1–C6 were ruled by the user in conversation; C7 is a
    13	builder-side consequence; C8 is open. The design rewalk is where each ruling
    14	gets confirmed against the whole; until the spec is updated at that walk, this
    15	document governs these points.
    16	
    17	## C1 — The dedicated-identity rung is admitted early, as an account
    18	
    19	The spec names two triggers for its mechanical-closure rung: the audit ever
    20	firing, or the boss admitting it early. The user admitted it early
    21	(2026-08-09). Form chosen: a **dedicated GitHub account**, not the GitHub App
    22	or CI job the spec sketched — an App/CI relocates the gate into CI, which the
    23	spec scoped out; a plain account keeps the gate a local program. The account
    24	is one of the user's existing accounts, added to `nedschorus/nedschorus` as a
    25	**collaborator with write, not admin, and never an org owner**. Blast radius
    26	of a stolen or misused token: push commits to this one repository, nothing
    27	else — it cannot change protection, administer the org, or touch other
    28	repositories.
    29	
    30	## C2 — The Unix-user boundary is the enforcement, not GitHub
    31	
    32	GitHub restricts *accounts*; it cannot see which agent or process used a
    33	token. On one box, every process of one Unix user reads the same credential
    34	files (verified on this box: `~/.config/gh/hosts.yml`, mode 600, owner
    35	`nedlern` — every agent, subagent, and shell here is `nedlern` and reads the
    36	same token). Therefore: the main-capable credential is owned by a **dedicated
    37	Unix user** (working name `nedschorus-gatekeeper`), unreadable by agent
    38	sessions; agents invoke the gatekeeper through a **sudoers rule scoped to
    39	exactly that program**. This is what makes "agents never push to main"
    40	mechanical rather than instructed. Harness hooks and CLAUDE.md are context,
    41	never enforcement — the project has already ruled this class
    42	(instruction-file guard: a soft block by design; spec: "a python script does
    43	not read it").
    44	
    45	## C3 — Account layout amendment
    46	
    47	Amends the boss-ruled 2026-07-21 layout. Branch protection's push restriction
    48	on `main` moves from `NedLern` to the gatekeeper account **alone**. `NedLern`
    49	and `NedLerner` remain the two org owners — owner power stays with the user,
    50	never with anything an agent runs as. Requires an org owner to apply (agents
    51	cannot: verified, `ubuntu-claude` cannot even read protection settings).
    52	
    53	## C4 — Agent tokens are scoped, and issues are scoped rather than gated
    54	
    55	The agent credential on this box today is a **classic** token with `repo` +
    56	`workflow` scopes: full control of every repository the account reaches, plus
    57	the ability to land GitHub Actions workflow files — the capability-by-landing
    58	class [nedschorus#31](https://github.com/nedschorus/nedschorus/issues/31)
    59	singles out. Replace with a **fine-grained token scoped to
    60	`nedschorus/nedschorus`**: contents read/write (branch pushes stay open — the
    61	[nedschorus#45](https://github.com/nedschorus/nedschorus/issues/45)
    62	"push-less" ruling covers main only; verified by dry run that branch pushes
    63	already work), issues write, nothing else; `workflow` dropped (the repository
    64	has no workflows; nothing breaks).
    65	
    66	Issue work is **scoped, not gated**: agents legitimately read, create,
    67	comment, and edit as ordinary work; there is no invariant like "one writer to
    68	main" to protect, so a mediating program would re-implement the issues API
    69	and buy nothing. Discipline lives at the skill/hook rung (`ghi-write`,
    70	[nedschorus#13](https://github.com/nedschorus/nedschorus/issues/13), and the
    71	gh-rewrite hook of C6). What would move issues up a rung, recorded: wanting
    72	per-write provenance, or approval-before-close. Neither is true today.
    73	
    74	## C5 — Break-glass: an unlockable credential, never a standing ungated agent
    75	
    76	The failure the user named: something goes wrong and an agent is needed to
    77	fix it. Split by failure class:
    78	
    79	1. **Gate defects** — the gate's own history is the recovery path: any
    80	   historical version of the program is directly runnable
    81	   (`git show <good-sha>:scripts/git-gatekeeper.py > /tmp/gk.py`). Standing
    82	   invariant, adopted: **the gatekeeper stays one standard-library-only
    83	   file** precisely so this always works.
    84	2. **A landing the gate wrongly refuses** — the credential is *unlockable*,
    85	   not held: a sudoers entry that requires the user's password. An agent
    86	   prepares the exact command and asks; the user approves in the moment.
    87	   Ten-second recovery, no standing main access in any agent environment.
    88	3. **Credential expiry or protection misconfiguration** — org-owner
    89	   territory; the user's alone by C3. No agent bypass would help, because no
    90	   agent may hold owner power.
    91	
    92	Rejected: a permanently ungated agent — it would be the party running
    93	half-finished gate code against production while holding the credential that
    94	lets mistakes through, and gate *development* needs no access at all
    95	(slices 1–3 were built and 146-case-tested entirely against throwaway bare
    96	repositories, credential-free).
    97	
    98	## C6 — The cooperative tier: rewrite hooks, the check-in skill, teaching refusals
    99	
   100	Verified: PreToolUse hooks in the current harness can return `updatedInput` —
   101	seamless substitution of a tool call. The tier, and the split:
   102	
   103	- **`gh` calls rewrite seamlessly** (the user's gh-ned pattern): semantics
   104	  map one-to-one, agents keep their trained behavior, zero token cost.
   105	- **`git push` toward the nedschorus remote gets deny-with-exact-invocation,
   106	  not a rewrite**: a push carries none of the declaration (`--files`,
   107	  `--base`, `--message` map to nothing in `git push`'s arguments), and
   108	  auto-deriving the whole declaration would gut the intentionality the spec's
   109	  `unchanged-path` refusal exists to force. The one moment of friction lands
   110	  exactly where the design wants a deliberate act.
   111	- **The check-in skill front-loads the declaration** so the first call is
   112	  well-formed: `--files` relayed from the agent's own staging (the `git add`
   113	  of this design), `--base` computed as `git merge-base HEAD origin/main`
   114	  (the branch point GitHub used to compute for free), message passed through
   115	  verbatim (commit message ≈ PR description; the trained act).
   116	- **Refusals teach** as the final tier; every error names the exact fix.
   117	
   118	Division of labor, stated once: the agent contributes only what it already
   119	does by training — choose the files, write the what-and-why; the machinery
   120	derives or auto-fills everything else (base, session origin, digest, issue
   121	trailer form). Hooks are convenience and coverage, **never the boundary**
   122	(C2 is the boundary). A `conflict` refusal is deliberately loud: resolving
   123	one takes judgment about intent, the one thing the machinery must never fake.
   124	
   125	## C7 — Privileged invocations refuse the test seams
   126	
   127	`--repo` and `--remote` exist so the test suite can hand the program
   128	throwaway repositories (B3a) with no test-only code path. The moment the
   129	program runs under the credential-holding user, those same arguments are a
   130	privilege surface: an agent could aim the credentialed program at an
   131	arbitrary remote. Binding: **when running as the gatekeeper user, `--remote`
   132	and `--repo` overrides are refused** (named refusal; the remote is pinned to
   133	the canonical repository). Tests run unprivileged and keep the seams.
   134	
   135	## C8 — OPEN: cross-machine callers
   136	
   137	The gatekeeper reads declared content from the caller's worktree, which
   138	works only when caller and gatekeeper share a filesystem. Mac-side agents
   139	(CLI, Mac app) cannot be read from the Ubuntu box. Candidate shapes, none
   140	chosen: the caller pushes its branch first and the gatekeeper reads content
   141	from the branch ref (a real contract change — the request names a ref
   142	instead of relying on worktree bytes); or Mac agents do not check in
   143	directly and route work through the Ubuntu side; or a second gatekeeper
   144	installation on the Mac (two credentialed hosts — weakens C2's single-place
   145	property). Decide when a Mac-side agent first needs direct check-in; the
   146	spec's "invocable service" line anticipated the shape but did not design it.
